window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-6c7aa55b479f9cdd"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"rules\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">规则</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在文档 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code> 可获取对应 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>使用规则来控制 Codex 可以在沙箱之外运行哪些命令。</p> <p>规则目前属于实验性功能，后续可能发生变化。</p> <p><a id=\"create-a-rules-file\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">创建规则文件</h2> <p>如果在 Work Cloud 中使用本地计算机访问，请在 Agent Security 中通过 <code>rules</code> TOML 字段配置受支持的受管命令规则。这些规则适用于本地执行器；Work Cloud 容器则使用现有的 Work Cloud 策略。</p> <p>这里介绍的本地 <code>.rules</code> 指令适用于 Codex；不要据此认为 Work Cloud 的本地计算机访问会读取本地规则文件。有关受管 Work 环境中的适用要求，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">受管配置</a>和<a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">配置参考</a>。</p> <ol> <li>在某个当前生效的配置层旁边创建 <code>rules/</code> 文件夹，并在其中创建 <code>.rules</code> 文件（例如 <code>~/.codex/rules/default.rules</code>）。</li> <li>添加一条规则。下面的例子会在允许 <code>gh pr view</code> 在沙箱外运行前先询问。</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-python\">   # Prompt before running commands with the prefix `gh pr view` outside the sandbox.\n   prefix_rule(\n       # The prefix to match.\n       pattern = [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"view\"],\n\n       # The action to take when Codex requests to run a matching command.\n       decision = \"prompt\",\n\n       # Optional rationale for why this rule exists.\n       justification = \"Viewing PRs is allowed with approval\",\n\n       # `match` and `not_match` are optional \"inline unit tests\" where you can\n       # provide examples of commands that should (or should not) match this rule.\n       match = [\n           \"gh pr view 7888\",\n           \"gh pr view --repo openai/codex\",\n           \"gh pr view 7888 --json title,body,comments\",\n       ],\n       not_match = [\n           # Does not match because the `pattern` must be an exact prefix.\n           \"gh pr --repo openai/codex view 7888\",\n       ],\n   )\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>重启 Codex。</li> </ol> <p>Codex 会在启动时扫描每个当前生效配置层下的 <code>rules/</code> 文件夹，其中包括 <a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4?anchor=step-4-standardize-local-configuration-with-team-config\">Team Config</a> 位置和用户配置层中的 <code>~/.codex/rules/</code>。只有在项目的 <code>.codex/</code> 配置层受信任时，才会加载 <code>&lt;repo&gt;/.codex/rules/</code> 下的项目本地规则。</p> <p>你在 TUI 中把某条命令添加到允许列表时，Codex 会将规则写入用户配置层的 <code>~/.codex/rules/default.rules</code>，这样以后就可以跳过询问。</p> <p>启用 Smart approvals（智能审批；默认开启）后，Codex 可能会在你请求提升权限时建议一条 <code>prefix_rule</code>。接受前请仔细核对该规则的前缀。</p> <p>管理员也可以通过 <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=admin-enforced-requirements-requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code></a> 强制应用具有限制性的 <code>prefix_rule</code> 条目。</p> <p><a id=\"understand-rule-fields\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">了解规则字段</h2> <p><code>prefix_rule()</code> 支持以下字段：</p> <ul> <li><code>pattern</code>（必需）：用于定义要匹配的命令前缀，必须是非空列表。每个元素可以是：</li> <li>字面字符串（例如 <code>\"pr\"</code>）。</li> <li>字面值的并集（例如 <code>[\"view\", \"list\"]</code>），表示该参数位置可以匹配多个选项之一。</li> <li><code>decision</code>（默认值为 <code>\"allow\"</code>）：匹配规则时采取的动作。若多条规则同时匹配，Codex 会采用限制最严格的决定（<code>forbidden</code> &gt; <code>prompt</code> &gt; <code>allow</code>）。</li> <li><code>allow</code>：不再询问，直接在沙箱外运行命令。</li> <li><code>prompt</code>：每次运行匹配的命令前都先询问。</li> <li><code>forbidden</code>：不询问，直接阻止该请求。</li> <li><code>justification</code>（可选）：说明这条规则原因的非空、人类可读文本。Codex 可能会在审批提示或拒绝消息中显示它。使用 <code>forbidden</code> 时，如合适，请在理由中写明推荐的替代做法，例如可写为 <code>\"Use \\</code>rg<code> instead of </code>grep<code>.\"</code>。</li> <li><code>match</code> 和 <code>not_match</code>（默认值为 <code>[]</code>）：Codex 在加载规则时会验证这些示例。可用它们在规则生效前发现配置错误。</li> </ul> <p>Codex 判断是否运行某条命令时，会把命令的参数列表与 <code>pattern</code> 比较。在内部，Codex 将命令视为参数列表（类似 <code>execvp(3)</code> 接收的内容）。</p> <p><a id=\"shell-wrappers-and-compound-commands\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"shell\">Shell 包装器与复合命令</h2> <p>有些工具会把多个 Shell 命令包装成一次调用，例如：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">[\"bash\", \"-lc\", \"git add . &amp;&amp; rm -rf /\"]\n</code></pre> <p>由于这种命令可能会把多个操作隐藏在一个字符串中，Codex 会特别处理 <code>bash -lc</code>、<code>bash -c</code> 以及对应的 <code>zsh</code> / <code>sh</code> 形式。</p> <p><a id=\"when-codex-can-safely-split-the-script\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"codex\">Codex 可以安全拆分脚本的情况</h3> <p>如果 Shell 脚本只由以下内容组成：</p> <ul> <li>普通单词（不包含变量展开、<code>VAR=...</code>、<code>$FOO</code>、<code>*</code> 等）</li> <li>通过安全运算符（<code>&amp;&amp;</code>、<code>||</code>、<code>;</code> 或 <code>|</code>）连接的命令</li> </ul> <p>那么 Codex 会使用 tree-sitter 解析并拆分为单独的命令，再逐条应用规则。</p> <p>上面的脚本会被当作以下两个独立命令：</p> <ul> <li><code>[\"git\", \"add\", \".\"]</code></li> <li><code>[\"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"]</code></li> </ul> <p>即使你允许 <code>pattern=[\"git\", \"add\"]</code>，Codex 也不会自动允许 <code>git add . &amp;&amp; rm -rf /</code>，因为 <code>rm -rf /</code> 会作为单独命令评估，不会因前一条规则而自动放行。</p> <p>这样，当逐条评估命令是安全的，Codex 就能按命令评估；遇到其他情况时，则会采用保守处理，从而降低危险命令混入安全命令的风险。</p> <p><a id=\"when-codex-does-not-split-the-script\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"codex_1\">Codex 不会拆分脚本的情况</h3> <p>如果脚本使用了更高级的 Shell 功能，例如：</p> <ul> <li>重定向（<code>&gt;</code>、<code>&gt;&gt;</code>、<code>&lt;</code>）</li> <li>替换（<code>$(...)</code>、<code>...</code>）</li> <li>环境变量（<code>FOO=bar</code>）</li> <li>通配符（<code>*</code>、<code>?</code>）</li> <li>控制流（<code>if</code>、<code>for</code>、带赋值的 <code>&amp;&amp;</code> 等）</li> </ul> <p>Codex 就不会尝试解释或拆分它。</p> <p>在这些情况下，整次调用会被视为：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">[\"bash\", \"-lc\", \"&lt;full script&gt;\"]\n</code></pre> <p>随后，规则会针对这一次完整调用进行评估。</p> <p><a id=\"test-a-rule-file\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">测试规则文件</h2> <p>使用 <code>codex execpolicy check</code> 检查规则对某条命令的处理方式：</p> <pre><code class=\"language-shell\">codex execpolicy check --pretty \\\n  --rules ~/.codex/rules/default.rules \\\n  -- gh pr view 7888 --json title,body,comments\n</code></pre> <p>该命令会输出 JSON，显示最严格的决定和所有匹配的规则，包括匹配规则中的 <code>justification</code> 值。可添加多个 <code>--rules</code> 参数组合不同文件，并添加 <code>--pretty</code> 以便阅读格式化结果。</p> <p><a id=\"understand-the-rules-language\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">了解规则语言</h2> <p><code>.rules</code> 文件格式使用 <code>Starlark</code>（参见<a href=\"https://github.com/bazelbuild/starlark/blob/master/spec.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">语言规范（需联网）</a>）。它的语法类似 Python，但设计目标是安全运行：规则引擎可以执行它，而不会产生副作用（例如访问文件系统）。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"rules\">Rules</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>Use rules to control which commands Codex can run outside the sandbox.</p> <p>Rules are experimental and may change.</p> <h2 id=\"create-a-rules-file\">Create a rules file</h2> <p>For Local computer access with Work Cloud, configure supported managed command rules with the <code>rules</code> TOML field in Agent Security. These rules apply to local executors; Work cloud containers use existing Work Cloud policies.</p> <p>These local <code>.rules</code> instructions apply to Codex; don't assume Local computer access with Work Cloud reads local rule files. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a> and the <a href=\"#/article/a-c2332644faa13713\">Configuration Reference</a> for managed Work requirements.</p> <ol> <li>Create a <code>.rules</code> file under a <code>rules/</code> folder next to an active config layer (for example, <code>~/.codex/rules/default.rules</code>).</li> <li>Add a rule. This example prompts before allowing <code>gh pr view</code> to run outside the sandbox.</li> </ol> <pre><code class=\"language-python\">   # Prompt before running commands with the prefix `gh pr view` outside the sandbox.\n   prefix_rule(\n       # The prefix to match.\n       pattern = [\"gh\", \"pr\", \"view\"],\n\n       # The action to take when Codex requests to run a matching command.\n       decision = \"prompt\",\n\n       # Optional rationale for why this rule exists.\n       justification = \"Viewing PRs is allowed with approval\",\n\n       # `match` and `not_match` are optional \"inline unit tests\" where you can\n       # provide examples of commands that should (or should not) match this rule.\n       match = [\n           \"gh pr view 7888\",\n           \"gh pr view --repo openai/codex\",\n           \"gh pr view 7888 --json title,body,comments\",\n       ],\n       not_match = [\n           # Does not match because the `pattern` must be an exact prefix.\n           \"gh pr --repo openai/codex view 7888\",\n       ],\n   )\n</code></pre> <ol> <li>Restart Codex.</li> </ol> <p>Codex scans <code>rules/</code> under every active config layer at startup, including <a href=\"#/article/a-811aa7aeaf6b8cd4?anchor=step-4-standardize-local-configuration-with-team-config\">Team Config</a> locations and the user layer at <code>~/.codex/rules/</code>. Project-local rules under <code>&lt;repo&gt;/.codex/rules/</code> load only when the project <code>.codex/</code> layer is trusted.</p> <p>When you add a command to the allow list in the TUI, Codex writes to the user layer at <code>~/.codex/rules/default.rules</code> so future runs can skip the prompt.</p> <p>When Smart approvals are enabled (the default), Codex may propose a <code>prefix_rule</code> for you during escalation requests. Review the suggested prefix carefully before accepting it.</p> <p>Admins can also enforce restrictive <code>prefix_rule</code> entries from <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=admin-enforced-requirements-requirementstoml\"><code>requirements.toml</code></a>.</p> <h2 id=\"understand-rule-fields\">Understand rule fields</h2> <p><code>prefix_rule()</code> supports these fields:</p> <ul> <li><code>pattern</code> <strong>(required)</strong>: A non-empty list that defines the command prefix to match. Each element is either:</li> <li>A literal string (for example, <code>\"pr\"</code>).</li> <li>A union of literals (for example, <code>[\"view\", \"list\"]</code>) to match alternatives at that argument position.</li> <li><code>decision</code> <strong>(defaults to <code>\"allow\"</code>)</strong>: The action to take when the rule matches. Codex applies the most restrictive decision when more than one rule matches (<code>forbidden</code> &gt; <code>prompt</code> &gt; <code>allow</code>).</li> <li><code>allow</code>: Run the command outside the sandbox without prompting.</li> <li><code>prompt</code>: Prompt before each matching invocation.</li> <li><code>forbidden</code>: Block the request without prompting.</li> <li><code>justification</code> <strong>(optional)</strong>: A non-empty, human-readable reason for the rule. Codex may surface it in approval prompts or rejection messages. When you use <code>forbidden</code>, include a recommended alternative in the justification when appropriate (for example, <code>\"Use \\</code>rg<code> instead of </code>grep<code>.\"</code>).</li> <li><code>match</code> and <code>not_match</code> <strong>(defaults to <code>[]</code>)</strong>: Examples that Codex validates when it loads your rules. Use these to catch mistakes before a rule takes effect.</li> </ul> <p>When Codex considers a command to run, it compares the command's argument list to <code>pattern</code>. Internally, Codex treats the command as a list of arguments (like what <code>execvp(3)</code> receives).</p> <h2 id=\"shell-wrappers-and-compound-commands\">Shell wrappers and compound commands</h2> <p>Some tools wrap several shell commands into a single invocation, for example:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">[\"bash\", \"-lc\", \"git add . &amp;&amp; rm -rf /\"]\n</code></pre> <p>Because this kind of command can hide more than one action inside one string, Codex treats <code>bash -lc</code>, <code>bash -c</code>, and their <code>zsh</code> / <code>sh</code> equivalents specially.</p> <h3 id=\"when-codex-can-safely-split-the-script\">When Codex can safely split the script</h3> <p>If the shell script is a linear chain of commands made only of:</p> <ul> <li>plain words (no variable expansion, no <code>VAR=...</code>, <code>$FOO</code>, <code>*</code>, etc.)</li> <li>joined by safe operators (<code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code>, <code>;</code>, or <code>|</code>)</li> </ul> <p>then Codex parses it (using tree-sitter) and splits it into individual commands before applying your rules.</p> <p>The script above is treated as two separate commands:</p> <ul> <li><code>[\"git\", \"add\", \".\"]</code></li> <li><code>[\"rm\", \"-rf\", \"/\"]</code></li> </ul> <p>Codex then evaluates each command against your rules, and the most restrictive result wins.</p> <p>Even if you allow <code>pattern=[\"git\", \"add\"]</code>, Codex won't automatically allow <code>git add . &amp;&amp; rm -rf /</code> because it evaluates <code>rm -rf /</code> separately.</p> <p>This prevents dangerous commands from being smuggled in alongside safe ones.</p> <h3 id=\"when-codex-does-not-split-the-script\">When Codex does not split the script</h3> <p>If the script uses more advanced shell features, such as:</p> <ul> <li>redirection (<code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;</code>)</li> <li>substitutions (<code>$(...)</code>, <code>...</code>)</li> <li>environment variables (<code>FOO=bar</code>)</li> <li>wildcard patterns (<code>*</code>, <code>?</code>)</li> <li>control flow (<code>if</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code> with assignments, etc.)</li> </ul> <p>then Codex doesn't try to interpret or split it.</p> <p>In those cases, the entire invocation is treated as:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">[\"bash\", \"-lc\", \"&lt;full script&gt;\"]\n</code></pre> <p>and your rules are applied to that <strong>single</strong> invocation.</p> <p>With this handling, you get the security of per-command evaluation when it's safe to do so, and conservative behavior when it isn't.</p> <h2 id=\"test-a-rule-file\">Test a rule file</h2> <p>Use <code>codex execpolicy check</code> to test how your rules apply to a command:</p> <pre><code class=\"language-shell\">codex execpolicy check --pretty \\\n  --rules ~/.codex/rules/default.rules \\\n  -- gh pr view 7888 --json title,body,comments\n</code></pre> <p>The command emits JSON showing the strictest decision and any matching rules, including any <code>justification</code> values from matched rules. Use more than one <code>--rules</code> flag to combine files, and add <code>--pretty</code> to format the output.</p> <h2 id=\"understand-the-rules-language\">Understand the rules language</h2> <p>The <code>.rules</code> file format uses <code>Starlark</code> (see the <a href=\"https://github.com/bazelbuild/starlark/blob/master/spec.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">language specification (online)</a>). Its syntax is like Python, but it's designed to be safe to run: the rules engine can run it without side effects (for example, touching the filesystem).</p>"};
